"Arthur J. O'Dwyer" <ajo@nospam.andrew.cmu.edu> wrote:

>On Tue, 6 Jul 2004, Philipp Lucas wrote:
>> "Arthur J. O'Dwyer" <ajo@nospam.andrew.cmu.edu> wrote:
>> >The only hard part is unzipping the zipfiles ---
>> >DOS-based utilities often want to truncate or trash long filenames,
>> >which Nethack doesn't expect.
>> There are special provisions for DOS systems where patchlevel.h is
>> patchlev.h, y.tab.c is y_tab.c and so on.
>  Yes, and that's great if you're still using pure MS-DOS 6.22 (or
>earlier), but recently Microsoft has started adding LFN support
>either "on top of" the old DOS facilities, or "inside of" the OS
>itself (e.g., Microsoft Windows XP lets you use LFNs at the command
>prompt, although 'dir /-n' still displays 8.3 filenames).

Yes, of course. On /Windows/ systems where patchlevel.h stays
patchlevel.h and if you use a LFN aware programs, you should not use a
short file name archive program, unless all files are 8.3. This seems
more like common sense than a "hard part".

>  As an example, consider the file yy.tab.c, since you brought it
>up.  Depending on the OS, compiler, and runtime options, that file
>might turn out to be named any one of
>
>    yy.tab.c
>    YYTAB~1.C
>    yy_tab.c
>    yy.tab
>
>Unless you're very lucky (or very conventional;) , the default
>DOS/Win32 makefile will have one of the wrong names.  But a few
>iterations with a search-and-replace facility will solve any
>problems... at least until the next upgrade or patch cycle.

This also should not raise difficulties, as it is documented in the
Makefile. Going from the NetHack source to an executable really is not
much more than a few minutes of work for the user (if the compiler is set
up properly, of course).

